3. Functional Requirements
3.1 Global Filters (Slicers)
Objective

Allow users to filter entire dashboard using shared filter controls.

Functional Requirements

Users must be able to:

Add a global filter to dashboard.

Choose column for filtering.

Select filter type based on column type:

Column Type	Filter Type
string	Multi-select dropdown
number	Range slider
date	Date range picker
boolean	Toggle

Apply filter across all charts.

Clear individual filter.

Reset all filters.

Filters must:

Apply instantly.

Be reflected visually in charts.

Persist when dashboard is saved.

Filter State Structure
{
  "globalFilters": [
    {
      "id": "filter_1",
      "column": "Region",
      "type": "multi-select",
      "value": ["East", "West"]
    }
  ]
}
Rules

Filters must apply on computedDataset (after transformations).

Filters must stack (multiple filters combine with AND logic).

Removing filter must recompute dataset.

Acceptance Criteria

Selecting a region updates all charts.

Range slider updates charts instantly.

Date range filter works correctly.

Clearing filter restores previous state.

Filters persist after reload.

3.2 Cross-Filtering Between Charts
Objective

Enable charts to filter each other through user interaction.

Functional Requirements

When user clicks:

A bar in a bar chart

A slice in a pie chart

A point in scatter plot

The selected value must:

Apply as a temporary filter.

Update all other charts.

Highlight selected segment.

Clicking again must:

Remove selection.

Cross-filter state must:

Combine with global filters.

Use AND logic.

Be cleared independently.

Interaction State Structure
{
  "crossFilters": [
    {
      "sourceChartId": "chart_1",
      "column": "Region",
      "value": "East"
    }
  ]
}
Behavior Rules

Cross-filter applies only while selection active.

If multiple charts selected → stack filters.

If cross-filter conflicts with global filter → AND logic.

Source chart must show selection highlight.

Acceptance Criteria

Clicking bar updates other charts.

Clicking again removes filter.

Multiple selections stack correctly.

Charts re-render only when necessary.

No full dashboard reload.

3.3 Drill-Down (Hierarchy Support)
Objective

Allow users to navigate hierarchical data within a chart.

Functional Requirements

User must be able to:

Define hierarchy:

Date: Year → Quarter → Month → Day

Custom: Category → Subcategory

Enable drill mode per chart.

Click to drill into next level.

Provide “Drill Up” button.

Reset drill state.

Drill behavior:

Drill state affects only that chart.

Other charts remain unchanged.

Drill should work with active filters.

Hierarchy Definition Structure
{
  "hierarchies": [
    {
      "name": "DateHierarchy",
      "levels": ["Year", "Quarter", "Month", "Day"]
    }
  ]
}
Drill State Structure
{
  "drillState": {
    "chartId": "chart_1",
    "currentLevel": "Month",
    "path": {
      "Year": 2025,
      "Quarter": "Q1"
    }
  }
}
Acceptance Criteria

Clicking Year drills into Quarter.

Drill Up works.

Filters remain applied.

Drill state isolated per chart.

Dashboard reload restores drill state.

3.4 Chart-Level Filters
Objective

Allow each chart to have independent filters.

Functional Requirements

Per chart, allow:

Add filter specific to chart.

Top N filter (e.g., Top 5 by Revenue).

Sorting override.

Include/Exclude specific values.

Chart-level filters must:

Apply after global filters.

Not affect other charts.

Chart-Level Filter Structure
{
  "chartId": "chart_1",
  "chartFilters": [
    {
      "type": "topN",
      "column": "Revenue",
      "value": 5
    }
  ]
}
Acceptance Criteria

Top 5 filter shows correct results.

Chart-level filter does not affect other charts.

Sorting override works correctly.

Filters persist after reload.

3.5 State Management & Performance
Objective

Ensure interactivity does not degrade performance.

Requirements

System must:

Maintain unified state:

{
  "globalFilters": [],
  "crossFilters": [],
  "chartFilters": [],
  "drillState": {}
}

Debounce heavy recalculations.

Prevent full dataset reprocessing unnecessarily.

Use memoization for filtered datasets.

Avoid re-rendering charts that are unaffected.

Performance Acceptance Criteria

Interaction response time < 300ms for 50k rows.

No visible UI freeze.

Only impacted charts re-render.

Filter stacking works without performance degradation.

4. Non-Functional Requirements

Clean separation between:

Dataset

Transformation

Filtering layer

Visualization layer

Modular architecture.

Clear state synchronization.

Reproducible state restoration.

Error handling for invalid drill hierarchies.

5. Testing & Validation Checklist
Global Filters

Add multi-select → charts update.

Add range filter → charts update.

Clear filter → restore previous state.

Save & reload → filters persist.

Cross-Filtering

Click bar → other charts update.

Click again → reset.

Stack two chart selections → correct results.

Cross + global filter combine correctly.

Drill-Down

Drill Year → Quarter.

Drill Quarter → Month.

Drill Up works.

Drill state persists on reload.

Chart-Level Filters

Apply Top 5 → correct.

Add sorting override → works.

Chart filter isolated from others.

Save & reload → persists.

6. Completion Definition

Phase 2 is complete when:

Dashboard supports global filters.

Charts dynamically filter each other.

Drill-down works per chart.

Chart-level filters operate independently.

State persists across reload.

Performance remains smooth with 50k rows.

If any interactive behavior breaks state consistency, Phase 2 is incomplete.

Expected Outcome After Phase 2

Your tool should now feel like:

A fully interactive self-service BI dashboard comparable in interactivity (not modeling depth) to enterprise BI tools.